EMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID A Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID G Wild Type    PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    388C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    517C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    697C Wild Type  PTEN  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    800A Wild Type    RET  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_CASE_ID T Wild Type     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    524G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    733G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    742C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    743G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    817C Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    818G Wild Type   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    916C Wild Type    INTERPRETATION     POSITIVE for mutation s  in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     COMMENT   This test may not detect mutations in the assayed loci that occur at low  frequencies and will not detect mutations outside of the targeted loci     TEST INFORMATION   In recent years  major efforts to define the molecular causes of cancer have  revealed a wide number of genetic aberrations  A small subset of these defects   usually referred to as drivers   is frequently present across cancer types and  appears to be essential for oncogenesis and tumor progression  New drugs  developed to selectively target such cancer promoting pathways  are starting to  complement the more conventional therapeutic approaches  While the clinical  benefit observed with some targeted agents is encouraging  it has become clear  that for such strategies to be successful  it will be necessary to identify the  patient population carrying the genetic abnormalities targeted by each drug     We have developed a multiplexed allele specific assay to detect somatic  mutations in tumor DNA extracted from formalin fixed paraffin embedded samples   Dias Santagata  REMOVED_CASE_ID    The SNaPshot Version 4 clinical test detects common  mutations in 23 cancer genes  The SNaPshot multiplex assay tests for somatic  variants at 90 different loci  Briefly  multiplex PCR using tumor DNA is  followed by mutation detection using single base extension sequencing technology   SNaPshot  Applied Biosystems   In addition  a separate PCR reaction using  primers flanking EGFR exon 19  EGFR exon 20 and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  exon 20 are used to detect  in frame activating insertions or deletions in EGFR and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  that may not be  detected using SNaPshot  The mutations are visualized using an ABI  REMOVED_CASE_ID  DNA  analyzer  The average analytic sensitivity of detecting mutations in this assay  has been established in our laboratory at approximately 5  mutant allele     REFERENCES   Dias Santagata D  Akhavanfard S  David SS  Vernovsky K  Kuhlmann G  Boisvert SL   Stubbs H  McDermott U  Settleman J  Kwak EL  Clark JW  Isakoff SJ  Sequist LV   Engelman JA  Lynch TJ  Haber DA  Louis DN  Ellisen LW  Borger DR  Iafrate AJ    REMOVED_CASE_ID   Rapid targeted mutational analysis of human tumours  a clinical platform  to guide personalized cancer medicine  EMBO Mol Med  2 5  146 58     This test was developed  and its performance characteristics were determined by  the MGH Center for Integrated Diagnostics  It has not been approved by the U S   Food and Drug Administration  The FDA has determined that such clearance is not  necessary  This test is used for clinical purposes  Pursuant to the requirements  of CLIA 88  this laboratory has established and verified the test accuracy and  precision     Questions concerning these values should be addressed to Dr  A  John Iafrate at   617 726  REMOVED_CASE_ID   MGH internal address  Center for Integrated Diagnostics   Massachusetts General Hospital  55 Fruit St  Jackson  REMOVED_CASE_ID   Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID           Electronically Signed Out By  Long Phi Le MD  PhD         PART   Molecular test for SNAPSHOT       report_end  , Positive.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Specimen Type  Consult slide s   Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  Ordering Provider  MONICA M BERTAGNOLLI M D   CASE  BS 04  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   PATIENT  M  JOHNSON  Resident  BRIAN S  CHANG  M D   PH D   Pathologist  SUSAN LESTER  M D   PH D   PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   CONSULT SLIDES FROM BETH ISRAEL DEACONESS MEDICAL CENTER  BOSTON  MA   LEFT BREAST  12 00  CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR A MASS  04  REMOVED_CASE_ID   11 03 04    UNCLASSIFIED SPINDLE CELL SARCOMA  see note below    Immunoperoxidase studies were performed with the following results  not  reviewed    Keratin Positive  Vimentin Positive  Actin Focally positive   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Negative  Desmin Negative   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Negative  LEFT BREAST  TWO EXCISIONS  04  REMOVED_CASE_ID   11 24 04    LEFT INFRACLAVICULAR MASS   UNCLASSIFIED SPINDLE CELL SARCOMA  1 4cm  invading in fibroadipose tissue  adjacent to atrophic skeletal muscle  see note    The tumor is present at the margins   Biopsy site changes are present   Only rare ducts are present  consistent with breast parenchyma   Note  A paraffin block was previously sent for consultation to Dr  C  D   Fletcher and his consultation letter follows  previously reported as  BS 04  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      I agree with you that this is a difficult lesion  While  at first glance   areas of the lesion show scarring and xanthogranulomatous inflammation   presumably due to the prior therapy  nevertheless  there is a subtle but  undoubtedly atypical spindle cell proliferation  in areas dissecting through  the fat and in other areas having a remarkable pseudovascular growth pattern   Some parts of the lesion have a prominent collagenous stroma and the lesional  margin is diffusely infiltrative  In our hands  there is no  immunohistochemical evidence of specific differentiation   stains for  pan keratin  keratin  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   EMA   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  are all negative  In these  circumstances  I would regard this as an unclassified spindle cell sarcoma   morphologically low grade  likely radiation associated  As you know  a high  proportion of post radiation sarcomas are otherwise unclassifiable  In the  experience of our sarcoma clinic  tumours of this type often pose considerable  problems in terms of local control  perhaps due to a radiation field effect    rather than being associated with a high risk of metastasis  In these  circumstances  wide excision would be advisable    Dr  Fletcher also reviewed the additional HE slides received and his diagnosis  is unchanged   Additional immunoperoxidase studies were performed at Beth Israel Deaconess  Medical Center with the following results  not reviewed    PANK MNF 116 Positive  Keratin cocktail Positive   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Negative   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Negative  Factor VIII Negative  Desmin Negative  Actin Negative   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Negative  Estrogen receptor Negative  Progesterone receptor Negative   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu Negative  LEFT INFERIOR LATERAL INFRACLAVICULAR MASS   UNCLASSIFIED SPINDLE CELL SARCOMA   Tumor is present at the margin   Note  The specimen was received in multiple fragments  in aggregate  1 8cm   CLINICAL DATA   History  History of breast cancer  new left mass   Operation  Not provided   Operative Findings  None given   Clinical Diagnosis  None given   TISSUE SUBMITTED   Consult slides   GROSS DESCRIPTION   Received from Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center  Pathology Department   Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID   are sixteen  16  glass slides   One  1  slide is labeled   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and sublabeled  A LEVEL 1   corresponding  to a left breast  12 00  7cm from nipple  core biopsy for a mass  1 6 x REMOVED_DATE  cm  performed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology report  bearing the patient s name   Fifteen  15  slides are labeled   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and sublabeled  A  through  O    corresponding to a left breast excisions for masses A N  infraclavicular  5 4 x  4 5 x 1 7cm   O  inferior lateral infraclavicular  1 8 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm    performed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing  the patient s name  A paraffin block from this case was previously reported as  BS 04  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    By his her signature below  the senior physician certifies that he she  personally conducted a microscopic examination   gross only  exam if so stated   of the described specimen s  and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis es   related thereto   Resident Review by Susan Lester M D   Ph D   on REMOVED_DATE  Final Diagnosis by Susan Lester M D   Ph D   Electronically signed on  12 22  REMOVED_CASE_ID    report_end  , Positive.
